\section{Reachability}{

Elicited scenario targets are normative and routinely lie outside the envelope of
observed transition rates. \code{\link[=trans_rate_reachability]{trans_rate_reachability()}} quantifies this without needing a
target, and \code{\link[=solve_trans_rates]{solve_trans_rates()}} runs it as a precheck: it \emph{reports} how far outside
history each target is and only fails on gross violations (see \code{max_reachability_ratio}).
A solver that does not report how far outside history it went is actively misleading, so
the returned diagnostics are part of the result, not debug output.
}

\section{Solver dependency}{

The formulation is deliberately kept inside what \code{\link[lpSolve:lp]{lpSolve::lp()}} can express. Terms that
would usually be written as quadratic penalties (terminal fit, historic preference,
fairness across classes) are implemented as their L1 or minimax equivalents, which are
linear.

\code{lpSolve} is a \emph{suggested} dependency, because most of evoland never solves a linear
program: \code{\link[=trans_rate_bounds]{trans_rate_bounds()}} and \code{\link[=trans_rate_areas]{trans_rate_areas()}} work without it, while
\code{\link[=trans_rate_reachability]{trans_rate_reachability()}} and \code{\link[=solve_trans_rates]{solve_trans_rates()}} need it installed and say so if it
is missing.
}

